{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Implemented — bounded retry inside `acquire()`\n\nClaimed with a clean signed attestation (`verdict: certified`, head_sha `b13173bb7d`, 0 blocking).\nThe operator-approved `--force` fallback was **not** needed and was not used.\n\n**`_store/lock.py`** — `acquire()` split into `_acquire_once()` (one pass against one deadline,\nthe previous body verbatim) plus a retry wrapper. New `retries: int = 0` on `acquire()` and\n`write_lock()`; `write_path_retries()` reads `REBAR_LOCK_RETRIES` (default 2, clamped `[0,10]`,\nmalformed falls back rather than raising); `_retry_backoff_s()` is jittered\nexponential-with-cap (0.5s, 1.0s, cap 2.0s) reusing `gitutil`'s `_jitter` / `_backoff_sleep`\nseams rather than reimplementing them (plan-review A1 advisory).\n\n**Opted in — exactly five sites, 3/1/1:** `event_append.py` (the three write-path\n`write_lock()` calls), `txn.py` `_acquire_write_lock`, `push.py`'s locked commit-and-push.\nEverything else keeps fail-fast through the `retries=0` default with no edit at all.\n\n**`txn.py`** also stops swallowing the message: it now raises\n`CommandError(f\"Error: could not acquire lock — {exc}\")`, so the cumulative wait and the holder\nreach the caller instead of a bare generic string.\n\n### Two decisions worth recording\n\n**`retries=0` re-raises the ORIGINAL exception rather than rebuilding it.** Only the\nretries>0 path constructs a new `LockTimeout` carrying measured cumulative elapsed. That keeps\nthe historical single-budget message byte-identical, which matters because\n`test_lock_timeout_names_holder_7084.py:159` asserts **exact equality** on it and\n`completion_sidecar.py:57` documents the string. A cumulative-elapsed message everywhere would\nhave broken both for no gain.\n\n**The opted-in set is asserted by AST, not by line number.** `_opt_in_sites()` parses `src/` and\nasserts `{\"event_append.py\": 3, \"txn.py\": 1, \"push.py\": 1}`. This plan was itself blocked once\nfor citing drifted line numbers (my base was behind `origin/main`), so a call-site inventory\nkeyed on line numbers would rot the moment anything above it moves. It also verifies the\nnegative half — that compaction, fsck, the doctor, the reconciler and the best-effort sweeps\ndid NOT gain retries — which a grep over changed files cannot.\n\n### Verification\n\n`tests/unit/test_lock_retry_zebrafish.py` — 20 tests, all passing. The RED/GREEN pair is\nstructural rather than temporal: the same subprocess holder outliving one budget loses the write\nat `retries=0` and is ridden out at `retries=3`. Also covers no-double-append (the guarded body\nruns exactly once under contention), loud exhaustion with cumulative wait + one warning per\nexhausted pass, the claim shape, `txn` opt-in and message propagation, the `retries=0` default,\nzero backoff when uncontended, and the env override incl. clamping.\n\n`make lint` and `make typecheck` clean. `tests/integration/test_store_concurrency.py`,\n`test_lock_timeout_names_holder_7084.py` and `test_store.py` pass unchanged (51 tests green).\n\n`lock.py` is **797 lines** against the 800 cap — 3 lines of margin. Kept in place deliberately:\nthe retry cluster is well under the 100-LOC floor the module-size policy sets for a new file, so\nextracting it would trade a real rule for a nominal one. Flagging it as the next edit's problem."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786532872365116001,"uuid":"8c96f25d-652c-437c-a37e-00df845cb04a"}